<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucjan Żeligowski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lucjan Zeligowski</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucjan Żeligowski was a Polish general who in October 1920, in a staged mutiny secretly approved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. He proclaimed the Republic of Central Lithuania, nominally for the local Polish-speaking population, which was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1922. The seizure poisoned Polish–Lithuanian relations between the world wars. He later served as Poland's minister of military affairs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucjan-zeligowski.jpg" alt="Lucjan Żeligowski" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zeligowski_LOC_31862u.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lucjan Żeligowski was born on 17 October 1865 on a farmstead near Sikunie in the Oshmyansky Uyezd of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. His parents were exiled deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> for their part in the uprising, and he was raised by distant relatives. He attended a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, joined the Russian army as a volunteer and graduated from the military school in Vilnius in 1888. He fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904–1905. During the revolution he openly backed democratic reforms and was brought before a military court, but escaped conviction with the help of senior officers and the Polish Circle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-duma">State Duma</a>. In 1912 he joined the clandestine Union of Active Struggle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1915 he became a battalion commander in the Polish Rifle Brigade formed at Bobruisk. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 he helped organise the Polish Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and in 1918 began forming a Polish unit in the Kuban, later the 4th Polish Rifle Division, which fought alongside Denikin's Whites. Withdrawn to the new Second Polish Republic in April 1919, it became the 10th Infantry Division. A personal friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>, Żeligowski was promoted to general in the Polish-Bolshevik War and commanded the Lithuanian-Belarusian Front.</p><p>In October 1920 he took command of the 1st Lithuanian-Belarusian Infantry Division. On 8 October, after a staged mutiny, he took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> with his unit and on 12 October proclaimed the Republic of Central Lithuania, with Wilno as its capital. After elections he passed his powers to a parliament, which decided to submit the area to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He described himself as a Lithuanian who never ceased to be Polish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was promoted to three-star general in 1923 and in 1925 became Minister of Military Affairs. Piłsudski's May Coup ousted him, but he was soon returned to the post, and he retired the next year. In 1930 he published his memoirs of the war of 1920, and from 1935 he sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>. In his memoir published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1943 he condemned the annexation of Central Lithuania by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><p>After the invasion of 1939 he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he joined the Polish National Council. He died in London on 9 July 1947 and was buried at the Powązki Military Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucjan Żeligowski” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Soviet-Lithuanian Peace Treaty of July 1920 recognised Lithuanian claims to Vilnius, but in October 1920 Polish troops under General <b>Lucjan Żeligowski</b> seized the city in a staged mutiny.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Suwałki Agreement of 7 October 1920 left Vilnius on the Lithuanian side, but General <b>Lucjan Żeligowski</b>, on Piłsudski's orders, invaded the next day and took the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In October 1920, days after an armistice gave Vilnius to Lithuania, the Polish general <b>Lucjan Zeligowski</b> led troops in a staged mutiny, approved secretly by Pilsudski, and captured the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-central-lithuania">Republic of Central Lithuania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ašmiany, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.43,25.94&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Ašmiany on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5122 299 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lucjan Żeligowski"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5572 569h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5572 569h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.43,25.94&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
